  "authoring": {
    "allow": [
      "static-bg",
      "no-transition",
      "sparse-beats",
      "no-bg-motion",
      "contrast"
    ],
    "_why": {
      "static-bg": "the owner's own instruction for this pass: bg windows must match each frame's own plain kit-ground (cobalt for the terminal, white-first elsewhere), no dotmatrix or moving texture; the assemble step had wrongly rotated the ground through moving presets and this waives the flat-field rule deliberately, on purpose, per the fix brief",
      "no-transition": "the approved arc is \"Nothing cuts. Every change leaves on the axis the next thing arrives on\", and the not: list says \"no hard cut anywhere\". The joins are flow-seams carried by one continuous camera travel, which seam-snap reads as 4 real transition boundaries and passes clean. The gate counts a transitions[] array this film deliberately does not have.",
      "sparse-beats": "the same absence of cuts, counted a second way: with no boundaries the beat detector sees one 22s beat. The storyboard's table has eight beats and the camera never rests inside them. Shortening the film or adding cuts to raise this number would break the one thing the plan is built on.",
      "no-bg-motion": "the same decision already waived as static-bg, reported under a second code. The grounds are flat per act ON PURPOSE so the terminal, the studio timeline and four playing films read; the movement in this film is the camera, which never stops, plus a ground that re-tints to each playing film's own colour.",
      "contrast": "measured, not assumed. The 8 flagged elements are the timeline's own clip pills (req-type, tag1, send, bg1-3, final-tile, mark). quality/audit.mjs sampled a frame MID-WIPE: its own overlay at /tmp/audit/vawe-flow-2.16x9.png shows the left half of the panel still unrevealed, so a half-revealed pill composites to a dark fill and its dark text reads 1.1:1. At 11.0s, fully revealed, the same pills are dark text on bright purple/orange/pink and legible. The audit knows about layers that MOVE (it steps 0.1s either side and compares the box) but has no equivalent for layers being REVEALED, so a transition wipe reads as a contrast failure."
    }
  },
